Footnote: ⁎ [This depressed position of the tongue answers better for English *aw* in *saw* than for either *o* in *more* or *oo* in *poor*. For the *o* the tongue is slightly more raised, especially at the back, while for English *oo* the back of the tongue is almost as high as for *k*, and greatly @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
impedes the oral cavity. If, however, the tongue be kept in the position for *aw* by sounding ¶ this vowel, and, while sounding it steadily, the lips be gradually contracted, the sound will be found to pass through certain obscure qualities of tone till it suddenly comes out @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
clearly as a sound a little more like *aw* than *o* in *more* (really the Danish *aa*), and then again passing through other obscure phases, comes out again clearly as a deep sound, not so bright as our *oo* in *poor*, but more resembling the Swedish *o* to which it will reach if the tongue @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
be slightly raised into the A position. It is necessary to bear these facts in mind when following the text, where U is only almost, not quite = *oo* in *poor*, which is the long sound of *u* in *pull*, and is duller than *oo* in *pool* or French *ou* in *poule*.—*Translator.*]

